WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.231 --> 00:00:15.104
<v Daniel>You know, I mean, when we picture the future of work, there's this recurring image we've all been sold. It's like a pilot sitting in this pristine glass cockpit, hands completely off the yoke, while an algorithm just flies the plane</v>

2
00:00:15.224 --> 00:00:16.725
<v Daniel>perfectly through a violent storm.</v>

3
00:00:17.206 --> 00:00:20.809
<v Maya>Right. That comforting myth of total frictionless automation.</v>

4
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:30.733
<v Daniel>Exactly. We like to think the machine just takes over, solves all the complexity for us, and the human gets to just sit back, sip a coffee, and watch the screens.</v>

5
00:00:31.273 --> 00:00:41.477
<v Maya>Yeah, but then you step into messy, high-stakes, real-world fields, you know, like child welfare or hiring or criminal justice, and that autopilot image just kind of shatters.</v>

6
00:00:41.877 --> 00:00:50.540
<v Daniel>It really does. The human's hands are still tightly gripping the yoke, fighting the turbulence, and the machine is literally just a blinking light on the dashboard saying, hey, watch out.</v>

7
00:00:51.202 --> 00:01:03.490
<v Maya>And that blinking light, that represents the absolute frontier of the modern workplace right now. We are moving way past the theoretical debates about whether AI will replace us and into the gritty reality of human AI partnerships.</v>

8
00:01:03.651 --> 00:01:03.891
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

9
00:01:04.151 --> 00:01:19.081
<v Maya>I mean, the essential question isn't about replacing judgment anymore. It's about what happens when a machine prediction sits right alongside a professional's judgment. When nobody is forced to follow the machine, does anything actually get better? And crucially, whose judgment</v>

10
00:01:19.281 --> 00:01:20.222
<v Maya>does it actually help?</v>

11
00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:35.220
<v Daniel>So if you were listening to this deep dive and wondering how artificial intelligence is going to change your day-to-day job, or if you're trying to figure out how to prepare the next generation of professionals for this massive shift, this is the exact scenario you need to pay attention to.</v>

12
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:35.980
<v Maya>Absolutely.</v>

13
00:01:36.621 --> 00:01:51.309
<v Daniel>And to get to the bottom of this, we are looking at a stack of incredibly revealing sources today. Our primary focus is a brand new, just fascinating experiment from September 2026 by researchers Barron, Ghosh and</v>

14
00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:51.769
<v Daniel>Lombardo.</v>

15
00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:57.993
<v Maya>Yeah. Predictive risk scores in the public sector. Experimental evidence from child protection investigations.</v>

16
00:01:58.466 --> 00:02:13.411
<v Daniel>That's the one. And we're also pulling in a few background papers to really test the boundaries of these findings and frankly see where this entire model falls apart. Okay, let's unpack this, starting with the exact setup of this brand new experiment. Because in</v>

17
00:02:13.451 --> 00:02:16.872
<v Daniel>this deep dive, the design of the study is the whole point.</v>

18
00:02:17.187 --> 00:02:27.427
<v Maya>It really is. I mean, before we even touch the results, we have to understand the specific environment where this tool was introduced. It completely breaks the mold of how we usually think about workplace automation.</v>

19
00:02:27.768 --> 00:02:28.870
<v Daniel>Okay, set the scene for us.</v>

20
00:02:29.421 --> 00:02:44.105
<v Maya>So we are looking at Northampton County, Pennsylvania, over a very specific 14-month window. This is from December 18, 2023 to February 15, 2025. And the experiment covered 4,752 child</v>

21
00:02:44.186 --> 00:02:48.707
<v Maya>referral observations, encompassing 3,488 unique children.</v>

22
00:02:49.047 --> 00:02:57.189
<v Daniel>That is thousands of highly sensitive, incredibly complex cases. And how many human investigators were handling that volume?</v>

23
00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:58.070
<v Maya>Just 34. Wait.</v>

24
00:02:59.725 --> 00:03:04.408
<v Daniel>Really, just 34. That is a staggering cognitive load for 34 people.</v>

25
00:03:04.768 --> 00:03:05.429
<v Maya>It's massive.</v>

26
00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:15.855
<v Daniel>You are essentially asking a very small group of humans to process an avalanche of trauma and historical data, day in and day out, and somehow never make a mistake.</v>

27
00:03:16.315 --> 00:03:23.640
<v Maya>Right. And to manage that exact cognitive load, the county introduced a tool. It was an XGBoost machine learning model.</v>

28
00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.102
<v Daniel>Which in non-technical terms is what exactly?</v>

29
00:03:27.778 --> 00:03:39.541
<v Maya>Basically, XGBoost is a highly advanced pattern recognition system. They trained it on historical county records to figure out which combinations of factors usually led to a child eventually being placed in foster care.</v>

30
00:03:39.901 --> 00:03:40.561
<v Daniel>Okay, got it.</v>

31
00:03:41.021 --> 00:03:49.283
<v Maya>And based on that history, the model produced a simple five-level complexity score for each new case. It ranged from very low to very high risk.</v>

32
00:03:49.908 --> 00:03:58.146
<v Daniel>So let me guess the methodology here. They forced half the human investigators to use the tool and completely took it away from the other half to see who performed better.</v>

33
00:03:58.693 --> 00:04:05.779
<v Maya>Actually, no. The researchers took a much more elegant approach. The experiment was pre-registered and randomized at the family level.</v>

34
00:04:05.919 --> 00:04:06.539
<v Daniel>Oh, interesting.</v>

35
00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:20.530
<v Maya>Yeah. The algorithm was running in the background for every single case, but the only thing that was randomized was whether the human supervisor could actually see the algorithm's score on their screen. For the control cases, the screen literally just read, score not shown.</v>

36
00:04:20.982 --> 00:04:26.564
<v Daniel>Meaning the tool is generating a prediction for everyone, but only some supervisors get the privilege of looking at it.</v>

37
00:04:26.784 --> 00:04:39.988
<v Maya>Exactly. And here is the most crucial insight you need to take away from this setup. Every single piece of data, like every historical input the algorithm used to calculate that risk score, was already sitting in the family's case file.</v>

38
00:04:40.277 --> 00:04:41.998
<v Daniel>Wait, so the human already had access to it?</v>

39
00:04:42.219 --> 00:04:56.971
<v Maya>Yes. The experiment only changed how information was presented to the human, not what information existed. And on top of that, the algorithm recommended absolutely nothing. It automated nothing. The human professionals retained 100% full</v>

40
00:04:57.011 --> 00:04:57.992
<v Maya>discretion over the case.</v>

41
00:04:58.272 --> 00:05:10.760
<v Daniel>Wow. It's like having a sous chef who doesn't actually cook anything, and they don't bring a single new ingredient into the kitchen. They just walk in, point a laser pointer at a pile of ingredients you already bought, and say, hey, pay attention to this.</v>

