<v Daniel>Okay, break that down for me.</v>

57
00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:43.686
<v Maya>For that very high group, placement rose 0.46 standard deviations. And that's statistically significant with a p-value of less than 0.10. Wow. Moving a case into ongoing services rose 0.49 standard deviations. That one is</v>

58
00:06:43.746 --> 00:06:53.551
<v Maya>highly significant with a p-value of less than 0.01. And substantiating the abuse allegation rose 0.32 standard deviations with a p-value of less than 0.05.</v>

59
00:06:54.426 --> 00:07:01.068
<v Daniel>So they didn't just blanket increase their interventions across the board. They surgically intervened where the machine pointed the laser pointer.</v>

60
00:07:01.408 --> 00:07:01.889
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

61
00:07:02.489 --> 00:07:11.412
<v Daniel>But did this mean they ignored the other cases? Like if I'm spending all my time on the very high risk files, what happens to the families flagged as very low?</v>

62
00:07:11.972 --> 00:07:20.835
<v Maya>There was almost no change at all for the lower risk cases. The baseline level of care remained steady. The surge in action was purely concentrated at the top of the risk scale.</v>

63
00:07:21.211 --> 00:07:35.755
<v Daniel>OK, so the action clearly changed. The humans did more. But taking a child out of a home or opening an ongoing service case, that is a massive disruption. Did all this extra action actually help the kids or did it just create more bureaucratic trauma?</v>

64
00:07:36.316 --> 00:07:44.038
<v Maya>Well, let's look at the outcomes. The researchers used re-referrals to the child protection system within three months as their primary proxy for child safety.</v>

65
00:07:44.423 --> 00:07:51.165
<v Daniel>Because if a family is re-referred, it usually means whatever intervention happened the first time didn't solve the underlying danger, right?</v>

66
00:07:51.285 --> 00:08:02.708
<v Maya>Right. And under the algorithm's guidance, re-referrals fell by 3.8 points from a baseline of 25.8%. That is a 15% reduction in children returning to the system.</v>

67
00:08:02.908 --> 00:08:14.232
<v Daniel>Okay, but let me play the skeptic for a second. If I take a bunch of high-risk children out of their abusive homes and put them into the foster care system, of course they aren't going to get re-referred for abuse in their original home.</v>

68
00:08:14.652 --> 00:08:15.872
<v Maya>Because they aren't there anymore.</v>

69
00:08:16.152 --> 00:08:21.554
<v Daniel>Exactly. That's not necessarily a brilliant intervention. That's just a mechanical result of removal.</v>

70
00:08:21.814 --> 00:08:31.437
<v Maya>The researchers actually anticipated that exact critique. When you exclude the children placed in foster care from the data entirely, the reduction in re-referrals is essentially unchanged.</v>

71
00:08:31.905 --> 00:08:36.491
<v Daniel>Oh, wow. Really? Yeah. The drop in harm wasn't a mechanical illusion.</v>

72
00:08:36.912 --> 00:08:44.722
<v Maya>Something about the investigation itself, you know, the ongoing services, the heightened attention, it materially changed the trajectory for those families who stayed intact.</v>

73
00:08:45.072 --> 00:09:00.069
<v Daniel>Okay, but I still have a systemic concern here. You have 34 investigators. Suddenly, an algorithm is telling them to pay hyper-attention to certain cases, and they are doubling down on services and substantiations. Didn't they just get totally</v>

74
00:09:00.209 --> 00:09:04.074
<v Daniel>overwhelmed? I mean, you can't just magically create more hours in the day.</v>

75
00:09:04.698 --> 00:09:15.161
<v Maya>You'd think so, but overall recorded tool use and supervisory activity did not rise across the board. The targeting improved without creating a broad, crushing increase in supervisor workload.</v>

76
00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:17.022
<v Daniel>So they just worked smarter, not harder?</v>

77
00:09:17.662 --> 00:09:32.226
<v Maya>Pretty much. They essentially reallocated their existing cognitive bandwidth more efficiently. And importantly, there was no evidence that racial disparities widened as a result of using the tool. There was also no evidence that the control families, the ones where the score wasn't shown, were</v>

78
