<v Maya>harmed by sharing the same busy investigators.</v>

79
00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:49.618
<v Daniel>You know, here's where it gets really interesting for me. I want to know the psychology of why this worked. Did the algorithm score actually improve the investigators' beliefs about the case? Like, oh, I didn't realize this was so dangerous until the AI told me.</v>

80
00:09:50.279 --> 00:09:51.840
<v Daniel>Or did it just give them cover?</v>

81
00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:54.141
<v Maya>Cover? What do you mean?</v>

82
00:09:54.473 --> 00:10:08.763
<v Daniel>Well, think about it from the perspective of a public servant. You look at a file, you have a terrible gut feeling, but intervening is a massive, life-altering decision. It's going to invite legal scrutiny, parental anger, and a mountain of paperwork.</v>

83
00:10:09.483 --> 00:10:18.309
<v Daniel>Sometimes a professional just needs the bureaucratic cover of a hard data point, a machine saying very high, to justify acting on the instinct they already had.</v>

84
00:10:18.577 --> 00:10:28.319
<v Maya>That is a deeply human reality of organizational decision making. And to be scientifically rigorous, the paper explicitly cannot separate those mechanisms.</v>

85
