105
00:12:47.759 --> 00:13:02.157
<v Daniel>The algorithm is the racket. It complements the pro's existing judgment, their footwork, their form. It accelerates what they are already good at. But the beginner, they're still going to hit the ball into the net, no matter how much carbon fiber you put in their hands,</v>

106
00:13:02.738 --> 00:13:05.341
<v Daniel>because they don't have the baseline skill to utilize the tool.</v>

107
00:13:05.581 --> 00:13:20.532
<v Maya>It's a great analogy, but I urge extreme caution with that finding. The researchers could only estimate baseline skill for 19 of the 34 investigators. Drawing massive systemic conclusions from an N of 19 is</v>

108
00:13:20.572 --> 00:13:23.254
<v Maya>an inference, not a definitive scientific finding.</v>

109
00:13:23.614 --> 00:13:38.412
<v Daniel>Understood. But it brings us to this massive tension in the background literature. Let's look at the broader landscape of AI in the workplace. Why did the algorithm complement the investigators so well in Northampton County when other major studies show</v>

110
00:13:38.452 --> 00:13:41.754
<v Daniel>human discretion totally ruining algorithmic accuracy?</v>

111
00:13:42.094 --> 00:13:54.761
<v Maya>We have to explore the multistage challenge here. The sharpest complement to our main paper is an October 2026 working paper by Jason Barron, Will Dobby, and colleagues. They examined multistage systems in Michigan's child welfare system.</v>

