146
00:18:49.755 --> 00:18:51.777
<v Maya>superior statistical calculation.</v>

147
00:18:52.177 --> 00:18:53.098
<v Daniel>Oh, that makes so much sense.</v>

148
00:18:53.779 --> 00:19:05.589
<v Maya>Yeah. And in the Virginia judge case, the disagreement wasn't even about accuracy. It was about goals. The algorithm optimized strictly for preventing recidivism, but the judges prioritized a social goal of leniency for youth.</v>

149
00:19:06.249 --> 00:19:20.037
<v Daniel>So what does this all mean for the modern workforce? And if we zoom out, how does this reshape our understanding of what it means to be a professional in the age of AI, especially for institutions training the next generation, like universities, for instance?</v>

150
00:19:20.591 --> 00:19:35.498
<v Maya>It creates a massive imperative for higher education. We can no longer just teach facts because the machine already knows the facts. We have to shift to teaching judgment. Students need to learn when to trust a mathematical model, when to override it, and critically, how to go</v>

151
00:19:35.558 --> 00:19:39.220
<v Maya>out into the real world and gather the messy context the model cannot see.</v>

152
00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:50.185
<v Daniel>But how do you even prove someone has that skill on a resume? I mean, we have degrees for accounting, for engineering, but how do we credential this specific type of human machine judgment?</v>

153
00:19:50.790 --> 00:20:05.439
<v Maya>That is the open question facing universities right now. They will have to partner directly with the public agencies and employers who are adopting these tools, creating entirely new frameworks to measure and credential that exact judgment before a student</v>

154
00:20:05.599 --> 00:20:06.760
<v Maya>ever enters the workforce.</v>

155
00:20:07.221 --> 00:20:21.945
<v Daniel>Which brings us right back to that 19 investigator inference from Northampton County. The idea that the algorithm only helped the seasoned pros and did nothing for the beginners. If that holds true across other industries, who is most exposed in this new economy?</v>

156
00:20:22.645 --> 00:20:37.494
<v Maya>Based on that inference, it presents a severe risk for early career workers, adult learners and first generation students. They are at risk of being handed a machines score on day one before they have ever built the foundational professional judgment that</v>

157
00:20:37.594 --> 00:20:39.035
<v Maya>the algorithm is supposed to complement.</v>

158
00:20:39.799 --> 00:20:49.382
<v Daniel>Man, if the future of work isn't a cage match of humans versus machines, but rather machines accelerating existing human judgment, we have a massive design problem.</v>

159
00:20:49.542 --> 00:20:50.282
<v Maya>We really do.</v>

160
00:20:50.803 --> 00:21:05.770
<v Daniel>Because how do we build entry-level jobs and training programs where the algorithm doesn't accidentally become a crutch? If the AI is doing all the basic synthesis, how does a junior employee ever get the reps in to become a seasoned pro? We have to make sure the digital</v>

161
00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:10.423
<v Daniel>co-pilot doesn't prevent the human pilot from ever learning how to fly through the storm in the first place.</v>

162
00:21:10.924 --> 00:21:14.895
<v Maya>That tension right there is going to define the next decade of organizational design.</v>

163
00:21:15.376 --> 00:21:19.648
<v Daniel>Something for you to mull over until next time. Thanks for taking this deep dive with us.</v>
